Expectations of the native link's tests (tests/native-link.scala). The program is the one of
tests/nir/fixtures (tests/nir/src/Shapes.scala, main class fix.Shapes), linked for the target
x86_64-unknown-linux-musl against the jars of Scala Native 0.5.12 that tests/nir/digests.txt names,
the program's directory first on the class path and the jars after it in that file's order.

digests.txt   the SHA-256 and the lines of what Scala Native's own tools (tools_3 0.5.12) leave for
              that class path and main class in a debug build with the immix collector and its
              optimizer off, once as the build decides on threads (it links without them: the
              program starts none) and once told to link with them:
                linked   the definitions its link reaches, as its build dumps them (`dump`,
                         linked.hnir): each as nir.Show prints it, in the order of the names
                lowered  the same lowered to the runtime's object model, as its code generator
                         dumps them (lowered.hnir), in the same order
              They are Scala Native's bytes, never teq's: `teq nir --link` and `teq nir --lower`
              have to print the same.
linked.txt    of the first link's dump, the definitions of the classes tests/native-link.scala
              names, whole and as they stand there: what a failure is read against
lowered.txt   the same of the first lowered dump
entries.txt   the names Scala Native's build gives its link to start from (`ScalaNative.entries`)
              for that main class, without threads and with them, in its list's order: the order
              teq's link reaches them in (src/native/reach.rs's tests)
scala-collections.txt
              the hashCode of names, paths and positions and the iteration order of the Scala
              library's mutable.HashSet and mutable.HashMap after generated operations, as Scala
              Native's classes and scala-library 3.8.4 give them on the JVM: the tables teq's link
              keeps where Scala Native's iterates one (src/native/scala.rs's tests; its header
              says more)
diamond/      a diamond of traits linked at many depths, Scala Native's listings up to the depth its
              link ends at and teq's own past it (its README)

Scala Native's own result stands in the order of the JVM's hash tables, another at each run of its
build: the class ids, the trait ids and the fields' layout that the lowering writes come from that
order. teq's is by name (`Global.globalOrdering`) where Scala Native's comes from a hash table, but
for the fields of java.lang.Class and java.lang.String, whose layout the C runtime reads, and for
the signatures a class is called with, which stand in the order of Scala 2.13's mutable.HashSet, two
of one hash in the order the link added them in; teq's link keeps Scala Native's tables where it
iterates one, so that it adds them in that order too (tests/native-link/collisions holds programs of
such names).

The expectations are remade by driving Scala Native's tools on the JVM over the same class path
stage by stage, as `scala.scalanative.build.Build.build` does (`ScalaNative.link` with
`withDump(true)`, `withOptimize(false)`, `GC.immix`, `Mode.debug` and the target triple; linked
again without threads where the first link reaches no way to start one, or once with
`withMultithreading(true)`; then `ScalaNative.codegen`), with the link's result put into teq's order
before the code generation, and the JVM run with `-XX:+UnlockExperimentalVMOptions -XX:hashCode=2`
so that no table keyed by an object's identity orders anything by chance: two such runs leave the
same bytes. linked.txt and lowered.txt are the lines of the named classes taken out of linked.hnir
and lowered.hnir; entries.txt and scala-collections.txt are written by programs that call Scala
Native's classes and the Scala library's on the JVM. No file here is written from teq's output, but
for the two digests of diamond/digests.txt that name teq (diamond/README says why).
